A||||24||3.8T Fiero GT||Need your help!  Re: Import vs. domestic proof!||||1||
Z||000000||3.8T Fiero GT||10-28-2001||08:27 PM||||Ok guys,<BR>Well, I used to have an import and was on a board b4, and now guys are arguing with me now about domestic potential versus import potential! <P>Anyways, I NEED PROOF, video, written, etc...of a car, (better if its DOMESTIC) of a STREET LEGAL CAR OR STREETABLE doing LOW 9's or hgh 8's, not a drag ready car cause we know imports can BEARLY hit those times  and domestics kill it! haha<P>I was thinking Grand National or a GMC Cyclone SUV doing such times STREET LEGAL!<BR>Can anyone get me proof? Perhaps mustangs?<P>**ALSO..but less important than above**<BR>I read once a while back GM actually used A TYPE of VTEC (what honda now uses and lives on) on Cadillacs...I told this to Honda guys and they got so irritated and dont believe me...hahhahaa! I think GM used some other type of system, but it acted the same as VTEC...does anyone know of this? I know I read it but cant remember! Let me know if u can help me proove this too? <P>Much Thanx!!!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||TRiAD||10-28-2001||08:48 PM||||Do searches for Lingenfelter Corvette, Hennessey Viper, Vector M12, Vector W8, Saleen S7, Mosler MT900, etc...<P>These are all street-legal cars, some are production, some "tuners", but all are domestic street-legal cars that will sheer the doors off Ferrari, Lamborghini, Porsche, etc...<P>have fun!<P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||10-28-2001||08:54 PM||||It's all relative, really.  You'd just be wasting time to try and prove it one way or the other.  <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/icare.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cutlass207TDC||10-28-2001||09:12 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 3.8T Fiero GT:<BR><B><BR>I read once a while back GM actually used A TYPE of VTEC (what honda now uses and lives on) on Cadillacs...I told this to Honda guys and they got so irritated and dont believe me...hahhahaa! I think GM used some other type of system, but it acted the same as VTEC...does anyone know of this? I know I read it but cant remember! Let me know if u can help me proove this too? <P>Much Thanx!!!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually,  almost all cars have vtec.  All vtec is, is a variable valve timing.  Honda has vtec written all over the place on their vehicles because it is the only way they can acheive high horsepower 4cyl engines(250hp out of 2.2 liters,  S2000)<P>||||reg||12
Z||000004||Nashco||10-28-2001||09:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cutlass207TDC:<BR><B> Actually,  almost all cars have vtec.  All vtec is, is a variable valve timing.  Honda has vtec written all over the place on their vehicles because it is the only way they can acheive high horsepower 4cyl engines(250hp out of 2.2 liters,  S2000)<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>And which "almost all" cars is it that have VTEC?  I don't know of many cars that have any variable valve timing whatsoever...especially on both intake and exhaust, as the new honda motors do.  Care to share what make/model it is that has variable valve timing?  I'd be interested to hear...<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||WampusCat||10-28-2001||09:32 PM||||BMW calls it VANOS and it is on both the intake and exhaust valves.  Mazda uses VVT on the intake side of the 2001 and newer Miatas.  <BR>Audi 2.0-litre - continous inlet <BR>Audi 3.0 V6 - continous inlet, 2-stage exhaust <BR>Audi V8 - inlet, 2-stage discrete <BR>Ferrari 360 Modena - exhaust, 2-stage discrete <BR>Fiat (Alfa) SUPER FIRE - inlet, 2-stage discrete <BR>Ford Puma 1.7 Zetec SE - inlet, 2-stage discrete <BR>Ford Falcon XR6's VCT - inlet, 2-stage discrete <BR>Jaguar AJ-V6 and updated AJ-V8 - inlet, continuous <BR>Lamborghini Diablo V12 since SV - inlet, 2-stage discrete <BR>Mercedes V6 and V8 - inlet, 2-stage ? <BR>Nissan QR four-pot and V8 - continuous inlet <BR>Nissan VQ V6 since Skyline V35 - inlet, electromagnetic <BR>Porsche Variocam - inlet, 3-stage discrete <BR>PSA / Renault 3.0 V6 - inlet, 2-stage <BR>Renault 2.0-litre - inlet, 2-stage discrete <BR>Subaru AVCS - inlet, 2-stage ? <BR>Toyota VVT-i - continuous, mostly inlet but some also exhaust <BR>Volvo 4 / 5 / 6-cylinder modular engines - inlet, continuous <BR>Volkswagen VR6 - inlet<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Nashco||10-28-2001||09:54 PM||||Where'd you find all this info, off the top of your head, or a web page?  I'd like to check this stuff out, I had no idea it was so widespread, looks to be with a lot of European/Japanese companies more than us here in America.  If there's a web page you could post, I'd appreciate it; otherwise, I'm off to Google!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000007||WampusCat||10-28-2001||10:01 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 3.8T Fiero GT:<BR>I read once a while back GM actually used A TYPE of VTEC (what honda now uses and lives on) on Cadillacs...<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Cadillac used a concept called Variable Displacement in their "V-8-6-4" engines.  It was basically a V-8 that could switch off either 2 or 4 of the cylinders to save fuel when demand is low such as highway cruising.  When you need power, all 8 cyl. would fire.  In practice, it was pretty unreliable and they ditched it.  Ford is working with the variable displacement engines for their SUV's but I don't think it's in production yet.<P>(That VVT info is here: <A HREF="http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/lancia/58/technical_school/tech_index.htm#Engine" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/lancia/58/technical_school/tech_index.htm#Engine</A>  )<p>[This message has been edited by WampusCat (edited 10-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||3.8T Fiero GT||10-28-2001||10:43 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by WampusCat:<BR><B>BMW calls it VANOS and it is on both the intake and exhaust valves.  Mazda uses VVT on the intake side of the 2001 and newer Miatas.  <BR>Audi 2.0-litre - continous inlet <BR>Audi 3.0 V6 - continous inlet, 2-stage exhaust <BR>Audi V8 - inlet, 2-stage discrete <BR>Ferrari 360 Modena - exhaust, 2-stage discrete <BR>Fiat (Alfa) SUPER FIRE - inlet, 2-stage discrete <BR>Ford Puma 1.7 Zetec SE - inlet, 2-stage discrete <BR>Ford Falcon XR6's VCT - inlet, 2-stage discrete <BR>Jaguar AJ-V6 and updated AJ-V8 - inlet, continuous <BR>Lamborghini Diablo V12 since SV - inlet, 2-stage discrete <BR>Mercedes V6 and V8 - inlet, 2-stage ? <BR>Nissan QR four-pot and V8 - continuous inlet <BR>Nissan VQ V6 since Skyline V35 - inlet, electromagnetic <BR>Porsche Variocam - inlet, 3-stage discrete <BR>PSA / Renault 3.0 V6 - inlet, 2-stage <BR>Renault 2.0-litre - inlet, 2-stage discrete <BR>Subaru AVCS - inlet, 2-stage ? <BR>Toyota VVT-i - continuous, mostly inlet but some also exhaust <BR>Volvo 4 / 5 / 6-cylinder modular engines - inlet, continuous <BR>Volkswagen VR6 - inlet</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks, I KNEW IT! YOU are the man!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>||||reg||7
Z||000009||Nashco||10-28-2001||10:55 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by WampusCat:<BR><B>  Cadillac used a concept called Variable Displacement in their "V-8-6-4" engines.  It was basically a V-8 that could switch off either 2 or 4 of the cylinders to save fuel when demand is low such as highway cruising.  When you need power, all 8 cyl. would fire.  In practice, it was pretty unreliable and they ditched it.  Ford is working with the variable displacement engines for their SUV's but I don't think it's in production yet.<P>(That VVT info is here: <A HREF="http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/lancia/58/technical_school/tech_index.htm#Engine" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/lancia/58/technical_school/tech_index.htm#Engine </A>  )<P>[This message has been edited by WampusCat (edited 10-28-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I was under the impression that this setup merely shut of fuel to those cylinders and left them alone otherwise.  This is also supposed to be why Cadillacs can drive 50 miles at 50 miles an hour after losing coolant..because they limit combustion and allow air to cool the motor for a limited time.  Any idea?  Thanks for the link.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Sleeper||10-28-2001||11:50 PM||||i notice most of the domestics you're talking about are new cars, why am i not hearing anything about shoebox novas or late 60's cameros or chevelles or gto's etc, etc. any of these cars are street legal and you don't have too smog. so the sky is the limit performance wise. geez, all my buddies that are old car motorheads think my fiero is rice.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Fformula88||10-29-2001||10:28 AM||||You want an argument?  Lets take an example here... put your common import to be tuned in a garage next to a domestic.  Lets say an Integra GS-R or Type-S or something and a Mustang GT.  Now, to make that Integra fast, your probably going to do cold air intake, exhaust and headers, new MSD ingnition, maybe the bottle or a turbo, and maybe internal work (cams etc).  Now, lets do the same modifications to the Mustang engine.  Which do you think will be faster?  I'd safely bet the 'Stang would be faster.  Now, if you want to go top of the line, I remember a Hennessey Viper in Motor Trend that went 0-60 in 3.3 seconds.  I think it would be awfully hard to find any FWD import to pull that off.<P>I have nothing against the import tuners either, but I do not think there is a good argument against big displacement engines for all out performance, and performance potential.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Mr.Frosty||10-29-2001||02:15 PM||||I agree.The reason there is such a thing as v-tec is to make halfway decent power out of an incredibly tiny motor.Plus the highly touted hp\liter ratio.One point that seems to miss most ricers is when your car is heavy and your only pushing 135 v-tec hp,your not going anywhere fast.That's not to mention that DOHC V-tec motors are totally gutless in the torque department.I'll take a good ole' torquey pushrod motor any day of the week."There is no replacement for displacement.Period"<P>------------------<BR>85 GT Turbo intercooled||||reg||7
Z||000013||3.8T Fiero GT||10-29-2001||06:18 PM||||yeah true...<P>i wish GM would make a import style car, (A NEW FIERO HIN HINT! WINK WINK!)  that would have a nice 2.25+ size motor and a light body!  and race tuned...such a a TypeR or something, but it could do a nice 14.0 or sumptin!  <P>cause sorry, cavalier and sunfire just done cut it at all! haha<P>but again...its the majority market in North America that determines cars...||||reg||1
Z||000014||Cutlass207TDC||10-29-2001||08:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mr.Frosty:<BR><B>I agree.The reason there is such a thing as v-tec is to make halfway decent power out of an incredibly tiny motor.Plus the highly touted hp\liter ratio.One point that seems to miss most ricers is when your car is heavy and your only pushing 135 v-tec hp,your not going anywhere fast.That's not to mention that DOHC V-tec motors are totally gutless in the torque department.I'll take a good ole' torquey pushrod motor any day of the week."There is no replacement for displacement.Period"<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>i think GM did a pretty good job with the DOHC engines.  The 3.4L TDC v6 pumps out 210hp and 215 ft. lbs.  The ZR1 V8 had 405hp, i'm not sure on the torque but i think its near 400.  But i do agree with you that displacement was needed to reach those figures.<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000015||Standard||10-30-2001||02:53 PM||||The 3.4 TDC was de-tuned big time.  The W-41 Quad 4 put out 195 horsepower, way back in the early 90's.  Not too shabby for a domestic 2.3l 4 banger.<P>The 3.4 TDC can make well over 250 HP, but the weak GM FWD trannies of the time couldn't hold that kind of power consistantly.  So GM de-tuned them for fear of too many warranty problems.<P>Domestic does not equal low tech.. look at the LS1.  So what if it still has pushrods.. all aluminum, reverse flow coolant, coil-on-plug, 30mpg and 300+ horsepower.  And stock for stock, no import can hang with them.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">          <BR><FONT COLOR="red">1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed</FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1"><FONT COLOR="white">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="blue">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000016||filthyscarecrow||10-30-2001||10:06 PM||||i could swear the ZR1 engine was designed by lotus. i'll have to check on that, but i think that's the engine they used in their GT-1 elises when the twin turbo esprit engine was deemed illegal. they were allowed to use it because they had designed it. <P>personally, i'd rather have a little high revving 4 cyl that's easier to package. it's all about weight too, our Formula SAE car has 85BHP and can do 0-60 in about 3.5-3.7 sec. it's a japanese 4cyl. 600cc displacement, with a 20mm restrictor on the intake. the 98 car (honda cbr 600F2) will do it just a touch quicker because it has straight carbs. <BR>i've said this before, and i'll say it again: there is a perfectly good replacement for displacement. it's called a turbocharger. dno't believe me? ask BMW. they got over 1400 hp out of a 1.6L 4 cyl in 1986. they were able to make over 1000 hp for a 2 hour race. and i believe the block they used at the time was from a production M3. <BR>take the restrictions away from a NASCAR and an F1 car, spend a similar amount of money on each and which will go faster? what were we discussing again?<BR>...||||reg||1
Z||000017||Mr.Frosty||10-31-2001||05:49 PM||||Hey filthywhatever.I think we were talking about naturally aspirated motors earlier in the post.Did you see my sig? I love turbochargers,but I like big V8's better.<BR>P.S. on those indy motors,they have to be rebuilt after every race.Not very durable.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT Turbo intercooled<p>[This message has been edited by Mr.Frosty (edited 10-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||AutoXdbdragSE||10-31-2001||11:29 PM||||No there is no problem whith domestic engines.. it's just that the manufactures don't think the public want what they are selling, and in japan they do.  <BR>perfect example is our little car we have. in 1985 ponitac developed this little thing known as the DOHC 16V Superduty.  A normaly aspirated 375hp monster motor.  imagine if you put that thing on a turbo with nos.. i mean luducris amounts of power. how hard would it have been for pontiac to have made a detuned 200hp N/A 3.1L 4cyl fiero.  Sure it would of came with superduty options pacage that would of cost like 5,000 but it would of been worth it.. oh wait.. i'm sorry 200hp in a four cylinder fiero would be way faster than a vette.. and all the more embarissing because of half the cylinders.. now i know why pontiac didnt' do it||||reg||1
